To account for the multiple testing issue in the computation of genome-wide type-I errors, the significance threshold was obtained after a Bonferroni correction as follows:\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$-\text{log}10\left(\frac{0.05}{\sum_{i=1}^{nb chr}{number \,of\, independent\, tests}_{i})}\right),$$\end{document}-log100.05∑i=1nbchrnumberofindependenttestsi),
where the number of independent tests was computed as the sum of the number of independent tests for each chromosome. For each chromosome, this number was the number of principal components required to describe 99.6% of the genotype variability, obtained from a principal component analysis applied to the correlation matrix between genotypes of the SNPs on the considered chromosome (square root (r2) of linkage disequilibrium (LD) between each pair of SNPs, Gao et al. [25]). The resulting genome-wide threshold (4.5 corresponding to 1690 independent tests) was used to select significant associations for each type of analysis. In addition, a cut-off of 3 (chromosome-wide threshold) was used only to assess whether a significant region identified in one analysis was suggestive in another one.
